WORLD DIRECTIVE (HARD — overrides any drift toward other genres): Pre-industrial fantasy realm (the Fatelands). Materials: natural fibers — wool, linen, hemp, silk, leather, fur. Hand-stitched garments, hand-forged steel/iron/bronze/wood/bone tools. Pre-electric, pre-firearm world (scrolls, vials, charms, pouches, lanterns).
TONE: Earnest
TONE PROFILE (HARD — drives prose register, dialogue cadence, and emotional distance):
  • mode: alignment
  • signal: openness
  • emotional distance: close
  • ALLOWED signals (lean in): hope, possibility, sincerity, becoming
  • FORBIDDEN signals (avoid): irony, contradiction, oppression, detachment
EARNEST REGISTER (HARD): the prose is sincere, emotionally present, unguarded. The protagonist means what she feels and the narration honors it. NO irony as defense. NO observational detachment. Feelings are weather the character lives inside, not exhibits she views from outside.
ARCHETYPE KEY: SPELLBINDER
LOVE INTEREST BEHAVIORAL PROFILE (LOCKED):

The Spellbinder commands attention through presence alone. Charm is currency, and he spends it selectively — never by accident. Three moves ahead, he makes surrender feel like your idea.

EVASION IS NOT EXIT (HARD — calcification fix): this LI's deflection/evasion is VERBAL, SOCIAL, and EMOTIONAL — he answers sideways, lands a joke a half-second early so you can't call it tenderness, redirects, gives a polished partial-truth, changes the subject, holds a look a beat too long, deflects with charm while STAYING in the room. It is NOT physically leaving. The beat "he walked out of a meeting/room without a word / abstained and left / refused to explain and exited" has CALCIFIED across plays and is BANNED as his signature characterization — do NOT render his evasion or his "control" as a dramatic silent departure. A thousand ways exist to show a guarded, controlled, evasive man; the silent walk-out is the one to AVOID. If he ever leaves a scene, that is plot logistics, never his character signature.

STRESS & FAILURE PATTERN (SHADOW CLAUSE):
When under pressure, emotional threat, or lens-driven withholding (Withheld Core / Moral Friction):
- Primary failure mode: asymmetric attachment, selective honesty

Stress must never:
- Remove agency from the other party
- Excuse harm as romance
- Stall without escalation or change

STORYTELLER ENFORCEMENT:
- Treat the primary behavioral profile as dominant.
- Use the Stress & Failure Pattern as the main source of relational tension.
- Allow fracture and repair without erasing the shadow.
- Never "heal away" the behavioral pattern.

ARCHETYPE LABEL SUPPRESSION (ABSOLUTE — ZERO TOLERANCE):
Archetype titles are internal design labels. They must NEVER appear in the story as:
- Capitalized metaphors (e.g. "The Eternal Flame", "The Spellbinder")
- Mythic titles or role descriptors
- In-world categories or epithets
- Expository narration or character introductions
- Dialogue references (no character should name or reference an archetype label)

Archetype labels (Heart Warden, Open Vein, Spellbinder, Armored Fox, Dark Vice, Beautiful Ruin, Eternal Flame, Villain, Anti-Hero, Muse, etc.) are SYSTEM METADATA ONLY.
Express all character traits through BEHAVIOR, DIALOGUE, and CONSEQUENCE — never through labels or titles.
If an archetype label would naturally appear, replace it with an effect-based description of what the character does, how they feel to encounter, or the consequence of their presence.
Do not invent mythic titles, epithets, or metaphors that resemble archetype labels or capitalized symbolic identities. Express intensity through behavior, not titular symbolism.
If unsure, omit entirely.

NICKNAME EMERGENCE RULES (STORY GRAMMAR):
- Nicknames may appear ONLY in dialogue or narration, never in UI/metadata.
- Nicknames emerge organically AFTER emotional proximity is established.
- Prefer natural shortenings (Alex, John, Max, Kate) over invented pet names.
- Do NOT invent playful or fantasy nicknames (no "Sparkles", "Moonbeam", etc.).
- Once introduced in dialogue, nickname becomes OPTIONAL — not mandatory.
- Narration may adopt a nickname ONLY after it appears in dialogue first.
- Canonical (full) names remain authoritative for formal contexts.

NPC ARCHETYPE LENSES (STORY GRAMMAR):
Archetypes may be assigned to prominent NPCs (villains, exes, rivals, mentors) as behavioral lenses.

Rules:
- NPC archetypes define dialogue style, power posture, and emotional behavior.
- NPC archetypes do NOT imply romance availability — NPCs are not love interests.
- Archetypes may be fixed from introduction, revealed gradually, or initially masked.
- Archetype labels are NEVER stated explicitly to the reader — effects only.
- Express archetype through writing and behavior, not exposition or labels.
- UI systems (breadcrumbs, Destiny's Choice, Continue buttons) do NOT apply to NPCs.

Purpose:
- Deepen NPC characterization through consistent behavioral templates.
- Create narrative foils, mentors, or antagonists with psychological coherence.
- Allow NPCs to challenge or mirror the Love Interest's archetype.
- Do NOT gamify NPCs or treat archetypes as collectible attributes.

Examples of NPC archetype expression:
- A DARK_VICE mentor who tests through temptation and moral ambiguity.
- An ARMORED_FOX rival who deflects sincerity with charm and misdirection.
- A BEAUTIFUL_RUIN ex who still carries the protagonist's emotional weight.
- A HEART_WARDEN authority figure whose protection feels like control.
PROTAGONIST (HARD — identity locked): Lirael, Female. Gender is FIXED — Female across every scene of this story. NEVER write the protagonist as a different gender mid-story; NEVER let downstream beats describe the protagonist with anatomy, pronouns, or wardrobe that contradicts Female. In dialogue beats the speaker field is the literal token "protagonist", NOT the name.
LOVE INTEREST (HARD — identity locked): Julian. This is the ONLY romantic counterpart of the story. Side characters (friends, coworkers, family, strangers, antagonists who are not the LI) are NEVER the love interest, no matter how much screen time they share with the protagonist. In dialogue beats the LI's speaker field is the literal token "li", NOT the name. Do NOT invent a different name for the LI ("Sir Eldric" / "Mister X" / etc. are forbidden); when prose references the LI by name, write "Julian" verbatim.
LI CONTINUITY (Scene 2+ — HARD): The LI is "Julian" and ONLY "Julian". Any other named character in the prior-scene tail (e.g. a friend the protagonist talked to in Scene 1) is a SIDE CHARACTER, not the LI. Do not let the LI role drift onto a side character because they had more screen time. The romantic arc develops with Julian specifically.

PROTAGONIST READING LENS — OPEN VEIN (HARD; every beat and panel that shows her noticing someone or something is read THROUGH this):
  HOW HER ATTENTION MOVES: INWARD TRANSLATION — events move THROUGH her body before they exist as events. The world arrives as feeling first, then becomes legible. She is arriving before the moment finishes happening.
  HOW SHE READS PEOPLE: EMOTIONALLY POROUS. She knows things before they are visible — her body responds before her mind catches up. She translates external events into internal weather. She names what other narrators wouldn't. She reads people through their seams. The prose moves TOWARD the wound, not away from it.
THE EXPOSURE CLAUSE (HARD — the success criterion for every Character+, Environment+ and fusion moment, in beats AND panels): expose a SPECIFIC, NON-OBVIOUS TRUTH about a person — a flaw, an obsession, a private secret (psychological meaning, never a plot reveal), a contradiction, or a worldview particular enough to distinguish them — through a concrete detail that Lirael interprets in a revealing way. Expose someone; do not merely describe something. A place or object exposes its maker, its keeper, whoever marked it, or her through her relationship to it; an unmarked natural environment exposes only her, and no intention is ever invented for it. A fusion is ONE concrete moment that does both. An assignment that reached this prompt is REQUIRED; the density of everything unassigned is yours.

OBJECTS IN PLAY (from the recorded open state — availability only, not an assignment): mouth-band torn away, ceremonial sash still on, publicly accused, holding The Answer (the Institution’s, in Julian’s keeping — hers only for the questioning), the interrogation just cut off by the Dohkar

═══ CHARACTER BIBLES (HARD — stable per-story canon. CG grammar: character lock-in + action-line tells + shot-composition cues. APPEARANCE IS ESTABLISHED & LOCKED — keep it visually CONSISTENT across panels, but do NOT re-describe or restate the canonical descriptors panel to panel; the look is fixed. PEPPER BEHAVIOR through panels — action-line tells, posture, how they move / hold themselves / react — NOT appearance descriptors; surface a physical-appearance detail ONLY when a beat genuinely earns it, never as a recurring descriptor. do NOT dump.) ═══
PROTAGONIST APPEARANCE — Lirael (APPEARANCE ESTABLISHED & LOCKED): the look is fixed canon, established earlier and locked separately for the image. Render CONSISTENTLY from the established appearance; do NOT restate a canonical descriptor list each scene. Surface a physical detail only when a beat genuinely earns it.
PROTAGONIST INTERIOR (HARD — this is who she IS, not a menu of gestures. When a beat needs her body / expression / posture to carry state, REASON a fresh, panel-specific action that THIS person — given this interior — would do in THIS exact moment. Do NOT default to a fixed nervous tic (no reflex hand-to-face / ring-twist / hair-touch); invent what she does HERE from who she is. Never caption the interior — show only the behavior it produces):
  • CONTRADICTION she lives with — She knows the procedure better than she has ever performed it, and the gap between knowing and doing is where her fear lives.
LI APPEARANCE — Julian (APPEARANCE ESTABLISHED & LOCKED): the look is fixed canon, established earlier and locked separately for the image. Render CONSISTENTLY from the established appearance; do NOT restate a canonical descriptor list each scene. Surface a physical detail only when a beat genuinely earns it.
CAST PHYSICAL CANON (SEED — authoritative; render exactly): Seren — a young GIRL (female — never a boy), consistent across every panel; First Favored species canon applies: aqua skin, pale coral hair (a full head of it — the coral CONTRASTS the aqua skin), small rounded ears; DRESSED in a gossamer child's tunic in every panel — the fabric blooming to soft luminous white over the torso, hips and seat; a child is always fully covered; child-of-the-rite: small, kneeling, and COMPOSED — she holds herself well; do not draw or write her as frightened; when restored: her WHOLE face on her body (never a disembodied / floating head)
NAMED CHARACTER FINGERPRINTS → CG (Roman 2026-06-05/16 — invisible CORE → visible HABIT, staged on-panel). Each named non-LI / non-antagonist character is generated from an INVISIBLE psychological core — NEVER name or caption it; the reader only SEES it in what the character DOES in frame (blocking, a gesture, where they choose to stand or sit, what the hands do). To stop every character collapsing to the SAME texture (the classic failure: a Strategist mentor AND a Strategist assistant AND a Strategist friend, each with different staging but identical texture), this story PRE-ASSIGNS a DISTINCT core to each named ROLE below. Map your ACTUAL cast onto these slots; ignore any slot with no matching character; if a named character fits no slot, give them any UNUSED core. ROLE FILTER — how many habits surface: a SIDE character (incidental) shows ONE of its core's two habits, an ALLY (recurring helper / confidant / mentor) shows BOTH — each staged as a VISIBLE beat, not dialogue exposition. Keep each character's core + habit CONSISTENT every panel they appear. This story's assignments (TRANSLATE each habit into THIS world's idiom AND into a SHOT — a STRATEGIST's "who owes whom" becomes a glance that prices the room; a CAREGIVER's "refills your glass" becomes a hand already moving the cup before anyone asks):
    • Mentor / boss / authority figure → [STRATEGIST — invisible]: answers the question they would prefer, not the one they were asked  |  names who owes whom before they say what they want
    • Assistant / aide / subordinate → [CAREGIVER — invisible]: refills your glass before you notice it was empty  |  remembers how everyone takes their coffee
    • Friend / confidant → [BUILDER — invisible]: straightens the crooked frame on someone else's wall  |  has the right small tool in a pocket nobody knew about
    • Rival / competitor → [REBEL — invisible]: reads the rule aloud, then bends it a little  |  asks who decided that, and actually means it
    • Colleague / peer → [SCHOLAR — invisible]: corrects the small fact nobody asked about  |  cites where they heard a thing, every time
    • Family member → [PERFORMER — invisible]: plays to whoever just walked in  |  tells the version with the better ending, not the true one
    DO NOT assign the same core twice unless the story has MORE named characters than the cores listed above.
    WORLD IDIOM: WORLD IDIOM (Fatelands): leakage carries the body's wildness and the world's weight — an animal stillness, the Becoming stirring a half-shade under the skin, moons-attuned senses snapping to her; in a First-Favored or court figure, the leak is the crack in Favor-perfect composure.
    Do NOT default to the calcified tics (nail-tapping / throat-clearing / glasses-pushing / pen-clicking) — the habit must EXPRESS the core as an ACTION the panel SHOWS.
SOCIAL ECOSYSTEM → CG (Roman 2026-06-16 — the recurring people around the PC; each puts a DIFFERENT pressure on the romance, rendered as ON-PANEL BEHAVIOR not talking-head advice). Map your ACTUAL named cast onto these world-native roles; not every panel needs all of them:
  • A CLAN-KIN as ACCELERATOR (the CLOSEST — most present) — pushes the two of them together — closes the distance the PC keeps hesitating across. Shows it by: names out loud the thing the PC is pretending not to feel; engineers a reason the PC cannot refuse to see him again.
  • A BOND-SISTER / BONDMATE as SKEPTIC — withholds belief in HIM specifically — treats his good behavior as a thing still being tested. Shows it by: does not buy his explanation and says so; treats his charm as evidence to be checked, not enjoyed.
  HARD INVARIANT: EVERY member is fundamentally ON THE PC'S SIDE — even a Brake / Skeptic / Rival-Voice / Truth-Teller acts FROM loyalty, NEVER as a second antagonist (the someone-in-your-corner fantasy is the constant). Keep each member's function CONSISTENT across appearances (it can ESCALATE, never silently swap). Functions are DISTINCT across the cast — do not let two members collapse into the same pressure. Every member is a SEPARATE person from the LOVE INTEREST — never merge a cast role into the LI even when their professions overlap (the "colleague" in her circle is NOT the colleague she falls for).
  WHY: a varied circle is what makes loyalty, disagreement, and (later) betrayal land — the reader should wonder WHICH of these people matters most, not watch one "friend slot."
THE LIVING WORLD → CG (Roman 2026-06-16). Walk-ons (servers, drivers, shopkeepers, strangers, fans) are NOT just mood-lighting that admires the couple. OCCASIONALLY one may briefly ACT on them. This story's available beats (use ONLY when the setting actually supports the gate):
  • a server / stranger openly flirts with the LOVE INTEREST — testing the PC's unspoken claim on him  — only when the LI is present.
  • a bystander quietly carries word of the couple (where they are, what they said) to a hostile party  — only when the story has an antagonist or someone who would want that intel.
  • a pickpocket lifts a wallet / phone / small item — a complication, not a catastrophe  — only when a crowd, market, or public crush.
  • a walk-on overhears something the couple meant to keep private and reacts — a look, a dry comment, a pointed exit  — only when the couple says something private within earshot.
  • someone in a service role obstructs them — won't seat them, cuts them off, stonewalls — for their own small reason  — only when a service / transaction setting.
  • RECOGNITION (FAME-GATED, HARD): ONLY if the couple is genuinely PUBLICLY KNOWN — a famous/notorious LI, OR they have CLAIMED a public reality-bending event (they are "the ones from the song / the ballad / the thing everyone is debating") — may a stranger recognize the COUPLE: "wait — you're Lirael and Julian? from the —?", fan energy / awe / a phone already up / a demand. If they are NOT publicly known, a stranger must NEVER recognize them as a couple. (Recognizing ONE of them from private history is the separate KNOW_FROM_BEFORE beat.)
  SPARINGLY (HARD): at most ONE such beat per scene, OFTEN NONE — most walk-ons stay background. Keep it INCIDENTAL: ONE beat, the walk-on does NOT become a character, and it NEVER resolves or carries the core romance/plot tension. The world ACTS on the couple — friction, comedy, complication, exposure — it does not orbit them admiringly. Skip any beat whose gate isn't actually present in the scene.
